A package dependency solver
===========================

This example picks a version of every package in a small package index.  A
plan is consistent when it meets the root requirements and the requirements
of every version it picks.  The program states what a consistent plan is.
The runtime finds all of them.


1. What the example shows about functional logic programming
-------------------------------------------------------------

Non-determinism as values.  The plan in

    plan = [ (p, anyOf (versionsOf idx p)) | p <- packages idx ]

assigns each package anyOf its versions.  The call anyOf xs is a choice
among the elements of xs.  So the one expression plan means every
assignment at once.

Lazy generate and test.  The guard

    all (holds plan) roots && all (depsHold idx plan) plan

keeps the assignments that meet the roots and the requirements of every
chosen version.  A failed guard is not an error.  It produces no value.  The
function plans has no loop, no backtracking code, and no search state.  The
runtime does the search.

A conflict.  Version 2 of app needs web 3, and web 3 needs http 2.  Version
2 of app also needs db 2, and db 2 needs http 1.  No plan holds both, so no
plan picks app 2.  The program does not say so anywhere.  The result follows
from the requirements.


2. What the example shows about calling Curry from Python
----------------------------------------------------------

The index is Python data: a list of (name, version, requirements) tuples.
Each requirement is a (name, versions) tuple.  The driver go.py passes this
nested data to the Curry function plans.  curry.eval converts it with
curry.expr: tuples become Curry tuples, lists become Curry lists, and ints
become Curry Ints.

A package name is a Python str.  curry.expr converts it by the type the
parameter expects, a String, also when the name has one character.

The roots come from the command line.  Each argument has the form
NAME=V1,V2.  The default is app=1,2.

One call,

    curry.eval(Deps.plans, index, roots, converter='topython')

is a generator of consistent plans.  The converter turns each Curry plan
into a Python list of (str, int) pairs.  The scheduler does not promise an
order of the values across the backends, so the driver sorts the plans.  It
then picks the newest plan in Python with max.


3. How to run
-------------

Run "make stage" from the root of the repository first.  Then run

    ./run

The run script uses the C++ backend by default.  To pick a backend, set
SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS in the environment:

    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:py ./run
    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:cxx ./run

To try other roots, pass them to go.py.  Use the Python of the staged
install.  For example, on the Python backend:

    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:py ../../install/bin/python go.py app=2
    SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:py ../../install/bin/python go.py web=1,2


4. Expected output
------------------

The file expected.out holds the exact output of ./run.  The driver prints
the index and the roots first.  Then it prints the plans:

    plans (4):
      app 1  web 2  db 1  http 1
      app 1  web 2  db 1  http 2
      app 1  web 2  db 2  http 1
      app 1  web 3  db 1  http 2
    newest: app 1  web 3  db 1  http 2

Every plan picks app 1, because app 2 has the conflict described above.
The output is identical on both backends.

With the root app=2 the driver prints

    plans (0):
    newest: none


5. Limits
---------

A plan assigns a version to every package in the index, used or not.  The
guard forces one choice at a time.  It rejects a partial plan as soon as one
choice breaks a requirement.  So the work depends on how early the
requirements prune.  In the worst case nothing prunes, and the work grows
with the product of the version counts.

The index here has nine versions of four packages, or 24 assignments.  A
warm run of ./run took 0.4 s on the Python backend and 0.2 s on the C++
backend.  The first run also compiles the module.  It took about 10 s on
each backend.

Measured worst case, with three versions of every package and no
requirements: seven packages, or 2187 plans, took 2.6 s on the Python
backend.  Nine packages, or 19683 plans, took 1.4 s on the C++ backend.
Keep the Python backend at seven packages or fewer to stay well under 30 s.
